{
  "schema_version": 1,
  "list_id": "claude-opus-5-5",
  "run": 3,
  "simulation_id": "sim-02",
  "member_id": "eriksson-olle",
  "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B043",
        "laureates": [
          "Hidetoshi Katori",
          "Jun Ye"
        ],
        "citation": "for optical lattice clocks at the magic wavelength"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B007",
    "B004"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "My configuration has changed from B007 to B043. DMFT remains a profound and highly productive framework, but its status as a method with controlled limits leaves a more fundamental qualification than I gave weight to initially. Optical lattice clocks instead present a sharply bounded physical advance: the magic-wavelength lattice permits interrogation of many atoms without the central trapping light shift compromising the clock transition. The achievement has demonstrated durable, independently reproducible measurement performance and scientific reach. Katori and Ye remain the most defensible compact attribution for the defining concept and decisive experimental development, subject to the stated archival check.",
    "case_against": "The central reservation is attribution and boundary-setting. The citation must distinguish the magic-wavelength lattice-clock advance from the broader optical-clock and atomic-clock lineage, and historical review could show that another contribution is inseparable from the achievement. The case should rest on demonstrated precision measurement, not on a future redefinition of the second or on prospective applications.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "mehlig-bernhard",
        "point": "Mehlig argues that DMFT is controlled only in particular limits and is otherwise a highly successful approximation, whereas two-dimensional conformal field theory identifies exact universal structure.",
        "response": "I accept the force of the qualification concerning B007. Its continuing value for correlated-material spectra and electronic structure is unquestioned, but a final prize configuration should not require us to resolve whether a broadly used approximation is itself the discrete discovery. This has moved B043 ahead of B007 for me; B014 remains an important theoretical alternative but carries its own broad historical-attribution question."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "kroll-stefan",
        "point": "Kroll emphasizes that lattice clocks are independently reproducible, scientifically useful, and credibly attributable to Katori and Ye, while requiring a clear distinction from the wider atomic-clock lineage.",
        "response": "I agree, especially that reproducibility across laboratories is evidence of a mature physical capability rather than merely an exceptional apparatus. The lineage concern is real and should constrain the citation to the magic-wavelength lattice-clock breakthrough, but it is not a reason to defer recognition if archival review confirms that the proposed pair captures its indispensable conceptual and experimental contributions."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "johansson-goran",
        "point": "Johansson stresses that the strongest B043 case is the controllable many-atom magic-wavelength system, not improved clock performance or a promised standards change alone.",
        "response": "I concur. That formulation makes the physics sufficiently distinct from earlier atomic-clock work and gives the award a coherent centre: controlled suppression of the trapping-light shift enabled a new regime of precision spectroscopy and measurement."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether archival review supports limiting B043 credit to Hidetoshi Katori and Jun Ye without omitting an indispensable contributor.",
      "Whether the final citation can draw a sufficiently precise boundary between magic-wavelength optical lattice clocks and the wider optical-clock lineage.",
      "Whether the historical record for B007 ultimately establishes a more sharply delimited discovery and attribution than is presently evident."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "None apparent from the supplied profile."
  }
}
